.TH expr "" "Command"
.SH NAME
expr \- compute a command line expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBexpr \fIargument ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
expr -- compute a command line expression
Usage:	expr argument ...
Arguments:
	n	Any integer with optional sign
	string	Used with comparisons and 'len' operator
	+	Arithmetic operators (one of '+', '-', '*', '/', '%')
	!	Unary not
	-	Unary minus
	==	Relational operators (one of '>', '<', '>=', '<=', '==', '!=')
	&	Logical AND of previous and next expression
	|	Logical OR of previous and next expression
	len	Length of string given by next argument
	e1 : e2	 Set to the number of characters matching regular expression
		'e2' in string 'e1';  if 'e2' contains any '\(...\)' sequences,
		the result is the concatenation of the matched parts
	( e )	Parentheses for grouping
	{ e1 , e2 , e3 }
		Evaluate 'e2' if 'e1' is true, 'e3' otherwise; 'e3' defaults
		to '0' if missing
The result is written to the standard output.
.HE
Each
.I argument
is one token of an expression; separate command-line arguments
correspond to separate tokens, so operators must stand as their own
argument, surrounded by spaces.
.B expr
evaluates the expression and writes the result, followed by a
newline, to the standard output.
It exits 0 if the resulting value is true (a non-null string other
than
.BR 0 ),
1 if it is false, and 2 on a syntax error or overflow.
.PP
An
.I argument
that looks like an optionally signed decimal integer is treated as a
number wherever the operator requires one; otherwise it is compared
or concatenated as a string.
Integers are held as signed long values; no check is made for
overflow in the arithmetic operators.
.PP
Operators, in increasing order of precedence (operators listed
together bind equally):
.IP "\fB{ \fIe1\fB , \fIe2\fB , \fIe3\fB }\fR" 0.6i
Return
.I e2
if
.I e1
is true, otherwise
.IR e3 .
If the third argument is omitted, it defaults to
.BR 0 .
.IP "\fIe1\fB | \fIe2\fR"
Return
.I e1
if it is true, otherwise
.IR e2 .
.IP "\fIe1\fB & \fIe2\fR"
Return
.I e1
if both
.I e1
and
.I e2
are true, otherwise
.BR 0 .
.IP "\fIe1 \fIrel\fI e2\fR"
where
.I rel
is one of
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR <= ,
.BR >= ,
.BR == ,
or
.BR != .
Return
.B 1
if the relation holds and
.B 0
otherwise.
The comparison is numeric if both
.I e1
and
.I e2
look like integers, and a string comparison otherwise.
.IP "\fIe1\fB + \fIe2\fR, \fIe1\fB \- \fIe2\fR"
Add or subtract; both operands must be integers.
.IP "\fIe1\fB * \fIe2\fR, \fIe1\fB / \fIe2\fR, \fIe1\fB % \fIe2\fR"
Multiply, divide, or take the remainder; both operands must be
integers.
.IP "\fIe1\fB : \fIe2\fR"
Match string
.I e1
against the regular expression
.IR e2 ,
using the pattern syntax of
.BR ed .
If
.I e2
contains one or more
.B \e( ... \e)
subexpressions, the result is the concatenation of the text each
subexpression matched; otherwise the result is the number of
characters
.I e2
matched at the start of
.IR e1 .
A failed match yields
.B 0
in either case.
.IP "\fBlen \fIe\fR"
Return the length of
.I e
in characters.
.B len
is a reserved word, and must be given as its own argument.
.IP "\fB! \fIe\fR"
Return
.B 1
if
.I e
is false,
.B 0
if it is true.
.IP "\fB\- \fIe\fR"
Return the negative of the integer
.IR e .
.IP "\fB( \fIe\fB )\fR"
Group a subexpression; each of
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
must be its own argument.
.PP
Because
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR & ,
.BR | ,
and
.B *
are also special to the shell
.BR sh ,
they must be quoted or escaped in a shell command line.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ed ,
.BR sh ,
.B test
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B expr
exits 0 if the expression's value is true, 1 if false, and 2 if a
syntax error, missing operand, or arithmetic error was found; in the
last case it also prints a message naming the offending argument.
